SECTION 2: DESIGN & UX REQUIREMENTS
================================================================================

7. RESPONSIVE DESIGN (Mandatory for all UI projects)
    - Mobile-first CSS approach
    - Breakpoints: 320px, 768px, 1024px, 1440px, 1920px
    - Fluid typography (clamp() or CSS locks)
    - Touch targets minimum 44x44px
    - Accessibility: WCAG 2.1 AA minimum (contrast 4.5:1, focus indicators)
    - Screen reader support (ARIA labels, semantic HTML, alt text)
    - Reduced motion support (`prefers-reduced-motion`)

8. VISUAL SYSTEM (No Emojis — Rich Icons Only)
    - Icon library: Lucide, Phosphor, or Font Awesome (SVG preferred)
    - Font pairing: 
      * Headings: Inter, Geist, or Space Grotesk
      * Body: Inter, Source Sans Pro, or system-ui stack
      * Code: JetBrains Mono, Fira Code, or Cascadia Code
    - Color system:
      * Primary/Secondary/Accent with 50-900 shade scale
      * Semantic colors: Success (emerald), Warning (amber), Error (rose), 
        Info (sky)
      * Dark mode support with CSS variables or Tailwind dark:
    - Spacing system: 4px base grid (0.25rem increments)
    - Shadow system: Layered shadows (0, 1, 2, 3 depth levels)
    - Border radius: Consistent scale (none, sm, md, lg, xl, full)

9. LAYOUT & COMPONENTS
    - Design system: Atomic design methodology
    - Component library: Shadcn/ui, Radix, or Headless UI primitives
    - Layout patterns: Sidebar + Main, Dashboard grid, Card-based, Split-pane
    - Navigation: Breadcrumbs, Command Palette (Cmd+K), Tabs, Pagination
    - Forms: Validation (Zod/Yup), Error states, Loading states, Auto-save
    - Tables: Sorting, Filtering, Pagination, Column resizing, Row selection
    - Modals/Drawers: Focus trap, Escape to close, Backdrop click

10. ANIMATIONS & EFFECTS
    - Entrance animations: Fade + slide (duration: 200-400ms, ease-out)
    - Micro-interactions: Button press (scale 0.97), Hover lift (translateY -2px)
    - Loading states: Skeleton screens, Progress bars, Spinner variants
    - Page transitions: Shared element transitions where possible
    - Scroll effects: Parallax (subtle), Sticky headers, Infinite scroll
    - Performance: Use `transform` and `opacity` only, `will-change` sparingly
    - No layout thrashing — batch DOM reads/writes

================================================================================
SECTION 3: DEVELOPMENT PROCESS
================================================================================

11. CODE QUALITY STANDARDS
    - Type safety: Strict TypeScript (no any), Rust (no unwrap in production), 
      Python (type hints + mypy)
    - Linting: ESLint/Clippy/Ruff with zero-tolerance for warnings in CI
    - Formatting: Prettier/rustfmt/black with pre-commit hooks
    - Naming: 
      * Variables: camelCase (JS/TS), snake_case (Rust/Python), PascalCase (classes)
      * Files: kebab-case for components, PascalCase for classes
      * Constants: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE
    - Comments: JSDoc/Rustdoc/docstrings for all public APIs
    - Complexity: Cyclomatic complexity &lt; 10 per function
    - File size: Max 300 lines per file (split if exceeded)
    - DRY principle: Extract reusable logic to hooks/utils/traits
    - SOLID principles: Especially Single Responsibility, Dependency Inversion

12. CODE OPTIMIZATION
    - Bundle optimization: Tree shaking, Code splitting (route-based), 
      Lazy loading, Dynamic imports
    - Image optimization: WebP/AVIF, Responsive images, Blur-up placeholders
    - Font optimization: Subsetting, `font-display: swap`, Preload critical fonts
    - Memory: No memory leaks (cleanup subscriptions, event listeners)
    - CPU: Memoization (useMemo, useCallback), Virtualization for long lists
    - Network: Request batching, Deduplication, Compression (Brotli/gzip)
    - Database: Indexing strategy, Query optimization, N+1 prevention

13. SECURITY HARDENING
    - Authentication: JWT with refresh tokens, OAuth 2.0 / OIDC, MFA support
    - Authorization: RBAC (Role-Based) or ABAC (Attribute-Based), Policy engine
    - Input validation: Schema validation on ALL inputs (Zod, Joi, serde)
    - Output encoding: XSS prevention, Content Security Policy headers
    - SQL Injection: Parameterized queries ONLY (no string concatenation)
    - Secrets management: Environment variables, Vault integration, 
      Never commit secrets
    - Dependency scanning: npm audit, cargo audit, Snyk, Dependabot
    - Rate limiting: Token bucket or sliding window per user/IP
    - CORS: Whitelist origins, credentials handling
    - HTTPS: TLS 1.3, HSTS headers, Secure cookies (HttpOnly, SameSite)

14. PERFORMANCE TARGETS
    - Web Vitals: LCP &lt; 2.5s, FID &lt; 100ms, CLS &lt; 0.1
    - API Response: P50 &lt; 50ms, P95 &lt; 200ms, P99 &lt; 500ms
    - Database: Query time &lt; 10ms for 95th percentile
    - Bundle size: Initial JS &lt; 200KB gzipped, Images &lt; 100KB each
    - Lighthouse score: 90+ across all categories
    - Load testing: Handle 1000 concurrent users minimum

================================================================================
SECTION 4: BACKEND & INFRASTRUCTURE
================================================================================

15. DATABASE DESIGN
    - Schema: Normalized to 3NF, then denormalize reads if needed
    - Migrations: Versioned, reversible, tested in CI
    - Backup strategy: Point-in-time recovery, automated daily backups
    - Replication: Read replicas for reporting, Write replicas for HA
    - Connection pooling: PgBouncer / Prisma / SQLx pool

16. API DESIGN
    - RESTful: Proper HTTP methods, Status codes, Resource naming
    - Versioning: URL path (`/v1/`) or header-based
    - Documentation: OpenAPI 3.0 spec, auto-generated from code
    - Pagination: Cursor-based for large datasets, Offset for small
    - Filtering: Query parameters, Range queries, Full-text search
    - Rate limiting: Headers (X-RateLimit-Limit, X-RateLimit-Remaining)
    - Error format: RFC 7807 Problem Details JSON

17. REAL-TIME FEATURES (if applicable)
    - WebSockets: Connection management, Heartbeat, Reconnection logic
    - SSE: For server-push updates (lighter than WS for one-way)
    - Pub/Sub: Redis Pub/Sub or Kafka for event streaming

18. CI/CD PIPELINE
    - Pre-commit: lint, format, type-check, secret-scanning (gitleaks)
    - Build: Docker multi-stage builds, Layer caching
    - Test: Unit → Integration → E2E (parallel where possible)
    - Security: SAST, DAST, Dependency audit, Container scanning (Trivy)
    - Deploy: Blue-green or Canary deployment, Automated rollback
    - Monitoring: Health checks, Readiness/liveness probes
    - Git workflow: Trunk-based or GitFlow with protected main branch

19. CONTAINERIZATION & ORCHESTRATION
    - Dockerfile: Multi-stage, Non-root user, Minimal base image (distroless/alpine)
    - docker-compose: Local dev with all services (DB, Cache, Queue)
    - Kubernetes: Deployments, Services, Ingress, HPA, PDB
    - Service mesh: Istio/Linkerd for mTLS, traffic splitting (if enterprise)

================================================================================
SECTION 5: TESTING STRATEGY
================================================================================

20. TEST PYRAMID
    - Unit tests: 70% coverage minimum, Mock external dependencies
    - Integration tests: Database transactions, API contract tests
    - E2E tests: Critical user journeys (login → action → logout)
    - Visual regression: Percy/Chromatic for UI components
    - Performance tests: k6, Artillery, or Locust for load testing

21. TEST DATA
    - Fixtures: Factory pattern (faker.js, factory_bot, fake-rs)
    - Seeding: Deterministic seed for reproducible tests
    - Cleanup: Transaction rollback or test database per run

22. LIVE DATA TESTING
    - Staging environment: Mirror production data (anonymized)
    - Shadow mode: Run new code alongside old, compare outputs
    - Feature flags: LaunchDarkly or Unleash for gradual rollout
    - A/B testing: Statistical significance testing (p &lt; 0.05)

